Supervised sup- vs Unsupervised

Supervised learning:
maps inputs to a known target using labeled data—for example, predicting loan default or customer churn using past outcomes; we evaluate with metrics like F1 or AUC.
Learn from labeled examples to predict a target (y).

Unsupervised learning:
learning finds structure without labels—like clustering customers, detecting anomalies, or reducing dimensions with PCA; we judge it using silhouette score and, importantly, business interpretability.
Learn from unlabeled data to discover structure/patterns (no y).

I use Supervised when a labeled target exists and I need predictions
Unsupervised when I’m exploring data or building segments/features to feed into supervised models.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
• Supervised: “Show me inputs + the correct answer; I’ll learn to map X → y.”    Indioendent, dependent
• Classification (predict categories): Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, SVM, Neural Nets.
• Regression (predict numbers): Linear/Elastic Net, Random Forest Regressor, XGBoost Regressor.

• Unsupervised: “I only have inputs; I’ll group, compress, or spot oddballs.”
• Clustering (group similar items): K-Means, Hierarchical, DBSCAN.
• Dimensionality Reduction (compress features): PCA, t-SNE/UMAP (for visualization).
• Anomaly/Outlier Detection: Isolation Forest, One-Class SVM, LOF.
• Association Rules: Apriori, FP-Growth (market-basket).


• Do you have a target column today?
If YES then, it’s a Supervised.
If NO then, it’s an Unsupervised (to explore, cluster, reduce dimensions, or find anomalies).
• Is the goal prediction for each future record? It is Supervised.
• Is the goal insight/structure (segments, patterns, outliers)? It is Unsupervised.
• Want both? Start with Unsupervised (EDA/segments), then build Supervised models per segment
