|Regularization|

•	Regularization is a technique used to avoid overfitting in regression models.
•	It adds a penalty to the model when the coefficients become too large.
•	This keeps the model simpler and more stable, especially when you have many correlated or noisy features.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Ridge Regression (L2 Regularization)

•	Ridge Regression adds a L2 penalty = sum of squares of coefficients to the cost function.
•	It shrinks all coefficients towards zero but never makes them exactly zero.
  Ridge keeps all features but reduces their strength to avoid overfitting.

5. Lasso Regression (L1 Regularization)
•	Lasso Regression adds a L1 penalty = sum of absolute values of coefficients to the cost function.
•	It can shrink some coefficients exactly to 0 — performing feature selection automatically.

6. Elastic Net Regression
•	Elastic Net Regression is a type of Regularized Linear Regression that combines both Lasso (L1) and Ridge (L2) penalties.
•	It gives you the best of both worlds —
•	Lasso’s ability to remove useless features, and
•	Ridge’s ability to handle correlated variables.
•	Elastic Net = Lasso + Ridge
It both shrinks coefficients and sets some of them to zero.


Simple Summary
•	Before Elastic Net:
Model memorized data, overreacted to Engine & HP → unstable predictions.
•	After Elastic Net:
Model balanced both features, ignored useless ones, and made predictions closer to reality.
