10. Decision Tree Regression
•	Decision Tree Regression is a tree-based algorithm that splits the data into smaller regions based on feature values.
•	Decision Trees create clear, rule-based models but can overfit if too deep. Pruning helps.
•	Decision Tree Regression splits data into groups based on conditions, and predicts the average value in each group.
•	It behaves like a “if-else rule engine” that makes decisions at each node.


Think of it as a flowchart:
•	At each node → ask a question (like “Is Area < 1500?”)
•	Depending on answer → go left or right
•	Continue splitting → until each region has similar values
•	Output the average value in that final region

11. Random Forest Regression
  •	Random Forest Regression is an ensemble model that builds many Decision Trees and then takes the average of all their predictions.
  . Random Forest = Many Decision Trees working together to make one strong prediction.”
  •	It Reduces variance and overfitting.
•	It’s like asking 100 experts and taking their average opinion — reducing errors and bias. 
•	Random Forest gives high accuracy and stability, suitable for large, complex datasets

12. XGBoost Regression
•	XGBoost (Extreme Gradient Boosting) is an advanced ensemble regression technique that builds multiple Decision Trees sequentially, not in parallel (like Random Forest).
•	Each new tree tries to correct the errors made by the previous trees — this is called boosting.
•	It uses gradient descent optimization to minimize the error at every step, making it fast, accurate, and efficient even on large datasets.
•	XGBoost Regression builds trees one after another —
each new tree learns from the mistakes of previous trees.
•	The final prediction is a powerful combination of all trees, giving excellent performance and accuracy.


In simple words:
•	Each tree learns from the mistakes of the previous ones — together they make a strong predictor.









